WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.180
Once Upon a Time in Hollywood might turn out
to be a career landmark film from one of the

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.800
most exciting directors of this generation.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.200
Here's everything you need to know before
you see Quentin Tarantino's newest movie.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.360
It's been nearly four years since Tarantino's
last film, The Hateful Eight, and anticipation

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.960
has been building for his next flick ever
since.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.900
Things really started to heat up in the summer
of 2017, when The Hollywood Reporter announced

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.420
that Tarantino's next project would be a film
set in Hollywood at the time of the Manson

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.340
murders.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:37.580
The film secured distribution from Sony in
the fall of 2017, and by January of 2018 Leonardo

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:41.320
DiCaprio had signed on to work with Tarantino
again on the project.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.900
The film spent the first six months of 2018
beefing up its cast as it headed into production.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.200
When it came time for a release date, Sony
initially aimed for August 9th, 2019, which

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.880
would mark the 50th anniversary of Sharon
Tate's murder at the hands of the Manson Family.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.480
By the summer of 2018, that release date had
changed to July 26th, with insiders noting

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.200
a release date in July would give the film
more time to play in front of summer audiences.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.860
As of early July 2019, a large number of critics
and fans have yet to see the film, but we

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.860
do have its initial reaction from the Cannes
Film Festival to rely on in terms of its quality.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.420
After its world premiere, Once Upon a Time
in Hollywood received a six-minute standing

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.660
ovation from the Cannes crowd, and reviews
coming out of the festival have been largely

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.460
positive.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:33.440
Though the ending, which Peter Bradshaw from
The Guardian called "startling and provocative,"

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.260
proved divisive, most critics were pleased
with Tarantino's visual style, the replication

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:43.440
of 1960s Hollywood, and the chemistry between
first-time scene partners DiCaprio and Pitt.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.280
So, while not everyone agrees, odds are this
is a movie worth looking forward to.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:54.580
Like Inglourious Basterds and Django Unchained,
Once Upon a Time in Hollywood is set against

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.060
a very particular historical backdrop.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.520
In both of those films, Tarantino took liberties
as he saw fit in an effort to best serve his

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.670
story, blowing up a whole plantation house
full of slavers in Django and flat-out machine-gunning

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.720
Hitler in the face in Basterds.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.860
With the press billing the film as an "alternate
history," fans have developed a theory that

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.330
Tarantino will continue his tradition of toying
with our expectations about what really happened

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:20.480
in our own past.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.291
The film is set in 1969 Hollywood, but Tarantino
didn't necessarily have to set his film in

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.760
our actual 1969 Hollywood.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.320
This is, conceivably, a world in which Sharon
Tate could survive the night she was supposed

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:37.730
to be murdered, in which Manson could get
his comeuppance, and in which Hollywood could

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.960
move forward into some different version of
the future.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:47.380
Like many Tarantino films, Once Upon a Time
in Hollywood quickly ignited a degree of controversy

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.600
after its premiere.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.980
At a press conference to promote the film,
a New York Times journalist asked Tarantino

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:57.870
why Margot Robbie, nominated for an Oscar
for her work in I, Tonya, didn't get more

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.200
dialogue in the film as Sharon Tate.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.380
The director was brief and dismissive with
his reply.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.880
"Well, I just reject your hypotheses."

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.680
"Right."

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.040
Robbie then stepped in to offer her own answer
to the question, saying that she was able

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.520
to express numerous facets of Tate in a nonverbal
way.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.290
"To really show those wonderful sides of her,
I think, could be adequately done without

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:23.040
speaking."

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.760
She added that it was "interesting" to be
able to explore the character even without

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:28.520
dialogue.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.760
"Rarely do I get an opportunity to spend so
much time on my own as a character, going

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.940
through a day-to-day existence."

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.840
Over his nearly three decades of filmmaking,
Tarantino has developed a kind of stock company

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.480
of frequent collaborators, and many of them
returned to work with him again for Once Upon

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:48.660
a Time in Hollywood.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.599
While Margot Robbie is a first-timer, both
DiCaprio and Pitt have worked with Tarantino

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.200
before, Pitt in Inglourious Basterds and DiCaprio
in Django Unchained.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:03.519
The film will mark Kurt Russell's third time
working with Tarantino, Bruce Dern's second,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.820
Michael Madsen's fourth, and stuntwoman and
actress Zoe Bell's sixth.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:12.890
Sadly, one frequent Tarantino collaborator
apparently ended up on the cutting room floor:

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:18.289
Tim Roth, who's appeared in three Tarantino
features and the anthology film Four Rooms,

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:22.120
was cast in the film but did not appear in
the Once Upon a Time in Hollywood cut shown

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.120
at Cannes.

